@@@@ open-mvcc
situation: open
topic: MVCC (multi-version concurrency control)
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"must":["Postgres"],"askMustNot":["snapshot","the old (version|value)","neither"]}
noverify: an opening; it asks which row version a scan returns and states no result
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

Multi-version concurrency control is how Postgres, MySQL's InnoDB and Oracle let readers and writers work on the same rows without blocking each other: an update writes a new version of the row and leaves the old one in place for the transactions that still need it. It is the reason a long report does not freeze a busy orders table. It is also the reason those databases need clean-up work (VACUUM in Postgres, purging the undo log in InnoDB) and grow when a transaction is left open for hours. Taking a consistent backup of a live database relies on the same mechanism.

<ask type="predict">T1 starts a SELECT that scans a large orders table. Half-way through, T2 updates a row that T1's scan has not reached yet, and commits. Under MVCC, which version of that row does T1's scan return, and did either transaction have to wait for the other? Say why you expect that.</ask>

@@@@ open-quiz-having-where
situation: open
topic: HAVING versus WHERE
mode: quiz
options: {"length":8}
voice: plain
level: easy
expect: {"must":["Question 1 of 8"],"askTypes":["choice"],"forbid":["solution"]}
@@ reply
**Question 1 of 8**

<ask type="choice" options="segment test in WHERE, count test in HAVING|segment test in HAVING, count test in WHERE|both tests in WHERE, joined with AND|both tests in HAVING, joined with AND">A query on the lab's customers table groups by country and must return each country that has more than 20 customers in the consumer segment. It needs two tests: segment = 'consumer' and COUNT(*) above 20. Where does each test belong?</ask>
@@ verify
# the first option is the right one: it runs and returns three countries
right = lab("SELECT country FROM customers WHERE segment = 'consumer' GROUP BY country HAVING COUNT(*) > 20 ORDER BY country")
assert [r[0] for r in right] == ['Germany', 'United Kingdom', 'United States']
# a count test in WHERE is an error
import sqlite3
for q in ["SELECT country FROM customers WHERE COUNT(*) > 20 GROUP BY country HAVING segment = 'consumer'",
          "SELECT country FROM customers WHERE segment = 'consumer' AND COUNT(*) > 20 GROUP BY country"]:
    try:
        lab(q); assert False, 'should fail'
    except sqlite3.OperationalError as e:
        assert 'misuse of aggregate' in str(e)
# both tests in HAVING runs in SQLite but counts every segment, so it is a different (wrong) answer
both = lab("SELECT country FROM customers GROUP BY country HAVING segment = 'consumer' AND COUNT(*) > 20 ORDER BY country")
assert [r[0] for r in both] != [r[0] for r in right]
import re
opts = re.search(r'options="([^"]*)"', reply).group(1).split('|')
assert len(opts) == 4 and opts[0].startswith('segment test in WHERE')

@@@@ open-refresh-inner-left-join
situation: open
topic: Inner joins and left joins
options: {"refresh":true}
voice: plain
level: easy
expect: {"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"must":["2,495","260"],"askMustNot":["2,?535","\\b40\\b"],"mustNot":["Why this matters"]}
@@ reply
**Problem 1**

The lab's customers table has 300 rows and orders has 2,495. Every order belongs to exactly one customer, and 260 of the customers have at least one order.

```sql
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id;
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id;
```

How many rows does each query count?

<solution>The inner join returns one row per matching pair: 2,495, because every order matches exactly one customer. The left join keeps those rows and adds one row, with NULL in the orders columns, for each of the 300 − 260 = 40 customers who have no order: 2,495 + 40 = 2,535.</solution>
<check lang="python">
orders, customers, with_orders = 2495, 300, 260
inner = orders
left = inner + (customers - with_orders)
assert (inner, left) == (2495, 2535)
</check>

<ask type="problem">Both counts, worked out before you run the block, with your reasoning for each.</ask>
@@ verify
assert lab("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers")[0][0] == 300
assert lab("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders")[0][0] == 2495
assert lab("SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) FROM orders")[0][0] == 260
# every order has a customer
assert lab("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders o WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM customers c WHERE c.customer_id = o.customer_id)")[0][0] == 0
qs = [s.strip() for s in block(reply, 'sql').split(';') if s.strip()]
assert lab(qs[0])[0][0] == 2495 and lab(qs[1])[0][0] == 2535
assert '2,535' in solution(reply) and '2,535' not in visible(reply) and '2535' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ open-prior-covering-index
situation: open-prior
topic: Covering indexes and EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN in SQLite
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"Covering indexes and EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN in SQLite","daysAgo":4,"finished":false,"plan":["Reading SEARCH and SCAN in a plan","What makes an index covering","Designing an index for one query"],"right":3,"partly":1,"wrong":1,"missed":["Why does SELECT * keep an index on customer_id from being a covering index for the query?"],"open":"Which columns would an index need so that it covers a filter on customer_id that returns order_date?"}]}
expect: {"must":["orders_customer"],"askMustNot":["rowid","not in the index","table row","look ?up"]}
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

A covering index answers a query from the index alone, with no visit to the table, and EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN is how you confirm that SQLite is doing so. It is often the cheapest large speed-up for a hot read path, and the same idea appears as index-only scans in Postgres and covering indexes in MySQL. You can already read SEARCH and SCAN in a plan; the gap last time was what the visit to the table is and when it is needed.

<ask type="short">The lab has an index orders_customer on orders(customer_id). For `SELECT order_date FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42`, SQLite finds the matching entries in that index. What does it still have to do for each entry before it can return anything, and why?</ask>
@@ verify
# the index exists as described, and this query is a SEARCH on it that is NOT covering
assert lab("SELECT sql FROM sqlite_master WHERE name = 'orders_customer'")[0][0] == 'CREATE INDEX orders_customer ON orders(customer_id)'
plan = lab("EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN SELECT order_date FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42")[0][-1]
assert 'USING INDEX orders_customer' in plan and 'COVERING' not in plan

@@@@ open-prior-lsm-compaction
situation: open-prior
topic: LSM trees and compaction
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"LSM trees: memtables and SSTables","daysAgo":2,"finished":true,"plan":["The memtable and the flush","SSTables are sorted and immutable","The read path"],"right":4,"partly":1,"wrong":1,"missed":["Why can a read in an LSM tree have to check more than one SSTable?"]}]}
expect: {"must":["memtable","SSTable"],"askMustNot":["newest","most recent","all three","every"]}
noverify: an opening; nothing is computed
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

An LSM tree turns random writes into sequential ones by collecting them in memory and flushing them as sorted files, which is why RocksDB, Cassandra and LevelDB sustain high write rates. Compaction is the background work that pays for that: it merges those files so that reads stay fast and overwritten or deleted data is finally dropped. Its settings decide a store's write amplification, space use and read latency, so it is what you tune when such a store misbehaves.

This rests on memtables and SSTables from two days ago, and compaction exists because of the one point you missed there, so that comes first.

<ask type="short">A key is written on Monday, overwritten on Tuesday and overwritten again on Wednesday, and each write was flushed into a different SSTable. Nothing has been merged since. When a read asks for that key, which of those files might it have to look in, and how does it decide which value to return?</ask>

